[
  "Welcome back to The Sound Above. I am Mike Hall. Today we enter The Human Communication Layer & Inclusion. Before code synthesis models and automated agents, developers met face to face to establish standards of craftsmanship. Today we rewatch Episode 13: Leon Gersing from SCNA 2012 (2012).",
  "Leon Gersing brought a disarming, radical message of empathy and vulnerability to SCNA 2012, challenging the elitism of the conference circuit and reminding engineers that software is a third of their lives.",
  "Why does the pursuit of technical excellence so often produce self-mockery and shame instead of joy? How do we replace hero worship with genuine empathy for people doing their best? What principles guided Leon Gersing when this conversation was recorded? How do those lessons inform the software systems we design today?",
  "Roll archive footage (YouTube ID: UIsjEX3DeDU). Watch for the unvarnished hallway exchanges, the emphasis on craft over speed, and the sheer enthusiasm of engineers taking responsibility for their software.",
  "As developers grapple with existential anxiety around automated code synthesis, Leon's message remains foundational: we are alive, we define our standard, let there be joy in solving problems. When implementation is cheap, verification and clarity become everything. We cannot accept the mirage of competence. Let us examine the terminal contracts directly.",
  "Thank you for joining today's session. Full transcripts, provenance notes, and companion research are available at just3ws.com. In our next broadcast, we explore Episode 14. Until next time, keep your feedback loops fast and your code humane."
]